WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:07.105
"Dental Implant Overdentures and Cognitive Function"

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.540
Systematic reviews
and meta-analyses

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.600
have found that tooth loss,
or periodontitis,

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.880
is associated with
both cognitive impairment

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:16.620
and dementia.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:20.580
Reverse causation might be
an intuitive explanation,

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.220
dementia leading to a decline
in oral hygiene,

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.640
but prospective studies
following people over time

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.320
have found that tooth loss

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.660
appears to predict
future cognitive decline,

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:34.200
and the more missing teeth,
the higher the associated risk.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:38.100
Genetic determinants
of elevated periodontitis risk

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.660
do not appear
to be associated

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.520
with the development
of Alzheimer's disease,

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.740
suggesting the association
may be due

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:46.320
to shared
or confounding risk factors.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.320
A poor diet
or low socioeconomic status

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:54.000
represent common pathways for
both tooth loss and dementia,

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.880
but even studies controlling
for confounding factors

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.420
found a connection
between tooth loss and dementia.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.500
However, one factor that wasn't
controlled for was intelligence.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.740
Those with higher IQ
are both more likely to floss,

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:10.560
and have fewer
tooth and gum problems

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.020
as well as have
a delayed onset

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:14.760
of clinically
detectable Alzheimer's

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.740
due to
a greater cognitive reserve.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:21.300
Are there any
potential causal mechanisms

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.160
beyond the chronic inflammation?

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:27.900
If you induce periodontitis
in mice with P. gingivalis,

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:31.200
the keystone pathogen
in human periodontitis,

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:35.340
the bacteria
ends up in their brains

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:36.900
and triggers an increase

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:40.260
in the Alzheimer's-associated
plaque protein amyloid beta.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.320
Since then,
evidence of the bacteria

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.900
has been found
in human Alzheimer's brains.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.180
Clinical trials
are now underway

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:50.400
to see if blocking
its neurotoxic effects

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.080
will have
any therapeutic benefit.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.920
A clue to
a stranger potential mechanism

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.200
was unearthed in
a subgroup analysis

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.380
of a tooth loss
and dementia meta-analysis.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:04.920
Only tooth loss
among those without dentures

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:07.920
was associated with the
later development of dementia.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:11.040
The same tooth loss
with dentures was not.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:13.380
How does that make any sense?

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.920
A review entitled
"Mastication for the Mind"

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:18.840
compiled a number of studies

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.020
suggesting that chewing,
mastication in medical speak,

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:25.500
is somehow important
for proper brain function.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:28.440
For example, the extraction
of teeth from aged mice

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:30.240
appears to upset
their memory and learning.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:33.540
But maybe they're just upset,
in pain, inflamed,

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:35.100
from having their teeth removed.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.620
A more convincing series
of experiments

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.600
compared mice
given food pellets

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:41.820
versus the same pellets
crushed into powder.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:45.660
Those eating the "soft" diet,
the powdered diet,

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:49.620
also suffered a loss in memory
and learning capacity.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:53.700
What does chewing
have to do with cognition?

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.820
Stick an ultrasound probe
on someone's head

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:59.700
and you can detect an increase
in cerebral blood flow

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:02.220
of the largest artery branch
in the brain

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:04.920
when they clench their jaw
or chew gum.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:06.840
In an fMRI machine,

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:10.020
brain scans show
enhanced cognitive arousal

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:12.480
when chewing even tasteless,
sugarless gum,

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:15.960
though translational studies
on the acute cognitive effects

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:17.760
of gum chewing
are contradictory.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:20.460
Maybe it's not the chewing,
though.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:22.980
Check this out.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.640
Noticing that people
missing molars

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:27.720
on one side of their mouths

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.780
have
asymmetrically-sized pupils,

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:33.720
a smaller pupil
on the missing molar side,

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:37.800
researchers tested whether
implanting dental crowns

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:39.000
to replace the missing teeth

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:43.140
could reduce the discrepancy,
and it did.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:46.620
The study, subtitled "New Teeth
for a Brighter Brain,"

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:50.520
suggested that a gap
in the sensation of teeth

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:51.960
pushing against one another

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.480
adversely affects
brain function.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:56.820
To put it to the test,

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.220
we'd want to randomize
people with missing teeth

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:00.240
to get dentures

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.760
and see if their cognition
improves over those who don't.

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:04.920
No such studies exist,

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:08.940
but there was an intriguing
pilot study performed.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:11.640
Ten toothless individuals,

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:14.460
9 out of 10 of them
cognitively impaired,

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:16.080
6 of them severely so,

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:18.960
were given conventional dentures
for a month

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:21.120
before being fitted
with overdentures,

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.640
which are snapped
into titanium implants

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:25.740
surgically screwed
into the jawbone.

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:27.540
The conventional dentures,

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:30.120
held in place by adhesives
and natural suction,

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:33.900
did nothing to significantly
alter cognitive function.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:37.080
But the ones securely attached
to implants in the bone,

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.180
presumably transmitting
the same kind

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:41.700
of chewing pressure sensations
to the nerves in the jaw

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:44.220
that natural roots
of teeth might,

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:46.080
had a dramatic effect.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:48.300
Nine out of ten
of the subjects

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:50.160
went into the study
cognitively impaired,

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:53.400
but 8 out of 10 left the study
cognitively intact.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:57.780
This suggests well-fitting,
secure dental prosthesis

00:04:57.780 --> 00:04:59.880
isn't just about improving
self-confidence,

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:01.920
social contact,
quality of life,

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:04.440
but proper brain functioning
as well.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:06.960
Of course, even better, though,

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:09.840
would be to preserve
the teeth you have.

